1c:T53d,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/animal-crossing-shared-time/#article","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/animal-crossing-shared-time"},"headline":"Animal Crossing and the architecture of shared time","description":"Why routines, roles, and custom play can make a digital place feel socially present even when players are apart.","image":["https://www.outbrick.site/blog/animal-crossing-shared-time.png"],"datePublished":"2026-09-04","dateModified":"2026-09-04","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"OutBrick Editorial Desk","description":"The OutBrick Editorial Desk turns player research, accessibility practice, and game-making lessons into useful, readable notes."},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OutBrick","url":"https://www.outbrick.site","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.outbrick.site/icon.png"}},"articleSection":"Social play","keywords":"Animal Crossing, social play, routines, community","citation":["https://doi.org/10.21153/psj2020vol6no2art970","https://doi.org/10.1037/a0019440","https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/expanding-social-mobile-games-beyond-the-device-screen/","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.241174"]}14:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$1c"}}]
15:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"OutBrick\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Journal\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Animal Crossing and the architecture of shared time\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/animal-crossing-shared-time\"}]}"}}]
16:["$","div",null,{"className":"article-body","children":[["$","section","a-place-that-waits",{"id":"a-place-that-waits","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"A place that waits"}],[["$","p","Comerford’s Persona Studies article examines Animal Crossing: New Horizons through interviews and a survey of nearly 2,000 players. The paper describes how players used the game for routine substitution, social connectivity, agency, and personas during a period of social isolation. Its historical context is specific, but the design observation travels: a place can feel social because it holds traces of people’s choices between visits.",{"children":"Comerford’s Persona Studies article examines Animal Crossing: New Horizons through interviews and a survey of nearly 2,000 players. The paper describes how players used the game for routine substitution, social connectivity, agency, and personas during a period of social isolation. Its historical context is specific, but the design observation travels: a place can feel social because it holds traces of people’s choices between visits."}],["$","p","A waiting world creates a different rhythm from a match that exists only while everyone is online. The player can contribute a small act, leave, and return to a world that has continuity.",{"children":"A waiting world creates a different rhythm from a match that exists only while everyone is online. The player can contribute a small act, leave, and return to a world that has continuity."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","animal-crossing-routines-2021",{"href":"#reference-animal-crossing-routines-2021","aria-label":"Jump to source: Comerford (2021)","children":["[",1,"]"]}]]]}]]}],["$","section","roles-make-room-for-people",{"id":"roles-make-room-for-people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Roles make room for people"}],[["$","p","Custom play gives players a role that the rules do not fully prescribe. One person becomes the gardener, another the host, another the collector, another the person who arranges a corner for visitors. These roles are not required to finish the game, which is why they can become meaningful rather than merely functional.",{"children":"Custom play gives players a role that the rules do not fully prescribe. One person becomes the gardener, another the host, another the collector, another the person who arranges a corner for visitors. These roles are not required to finish the game, which is why they can become meaningful rather than merely functional."}],["$","p","This is a form of autonomy. The system offers affordances, but the player decides what kind of person they want to be inside the space. Relatedness can then grow through the stories those choices make possible.",{"children":"This is a form of autonomy. The system offers affordances, but the player decides what kind of person they want to be inside the space. Relatedness can then grow through the stories those choices make possible."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","animal-crossing-routines-2021",{"href":"#reference-animal-crossing-routines-2021","aria-label":"Jump to source: Comerford (2021)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","motivation-2010",{"href":"#reference-motivation-2010","aria-label":"Jump to source: Przybylski, Rigby, & Ryan (2010)","children":["[",2,"]"]}]]]}]]}],["$","section","outbrick-as-a-shared-object",{"id":"outbrick-as-a-shared-object","children":["$L1d",["$L1e","$L1f"],null,null,"$L20"]}],"$L21"]}]
